/E <>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
272 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 705 0 R
/E ( Structural Violence: Structural violence is a form of violence in which certain social structures or institutions harm people by preventing them from meeting their basic needs or exercising their rights.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
273 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 706 0 R
/E (  Institutional Violence: Institutional violence is a set of systemic policies, practices, and beliefs employed by state institutions, corporations, or large organizations to exclude, exploit, or control vulnerable groups. Rather than physical force, it refers to the psychological, economic, or social pressure exerted on individuals through rules, laws, and organizational culture.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
274 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 707 0 R
/E ( Harmful Practices: Cultural, traditional, or religious practices that directly violate universal human rights standards and inflict long-term physical or psychological trauma. Within SOCHUM, the debate centers heavily on eradicating two prominent forms: Child, Early, and Forced Marriage \(CEFM\) and Female Genital Mutilation/Cutting \(FGM/C\).)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
