>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (      Gender inequality is not a contemporary aberration but the product of )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
284 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (      centuries of deeply entrenched, systemic patriarchal structures. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 172 0 R
/Pg 20 0 R
/A [713 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
286 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (Historically, legal, political, and economic frameworks worldwide were constructed around the male figure as the default citizen and head of household, effectively relegating women to the private sphere. This historic disenfranchisement manifested in the denial of fundamental rights, such as voting, property ownership, and independent financial agency, across most civilizations well into the 20th century.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (Within the framework of the United Nations, the recognition of these historical injustices led to pivotal milestones that SOCHUM delegates must reference. The adoption of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women \(CEDAW\) in 1979 established a landmark international bill of rights for women. This was later reinforced by the 1995 Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action, which remains the most comprehensive global visionary blueprint for achieving gender equality. Despite these historic treaties, the legacy of past discrimination continues to perpetuate modern disparities in power, institutional representation, and societal valuation.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (      b\) Forms of Gender-Based Violence \(GBV\))
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (Gender-Based Violence \(GBV\) is a global pandemic that crosses all social, economic, and national boundaries, serving as both a cause and a consequence of gender inequality. SOCHUM classifies GBV not as isolated criminal acts, but as a systemic human rights violation designed to enforce subordination.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
290 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 172 0 R
/T (        i. Physical and Domestic Violence)
/E (        i. Physical and Domestic Violence)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
291 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (Physical and domestic violence encompasses acts of bodily harm, sexual assault, and intimate partner violence occurring within the private sphere. This remains one of the most underreported forms of violence due to deep-seated cultural stigmas and the historical mischaracterization of domestic abuse as a "private family matter.". )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
292 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 173 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/A [714 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
293 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 173 0 R
/T (          ii. Psychological and Economic Abuse)
/E (          ii. Psychological and Economic Abuse)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
294 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 173 0 R
/E (Often invisible but deeply debilitating, psychological abuse involves coercive control, gaslighting, systemic isolation, and verbal degradation aimed at dismantling a victim's autonomy and mental well-being. This is frequently coupled with economic abuse, where perpetrators restrict a victim's access to financial resources, forbid them from seeking employment, or control their earned income. By inducing total financial and emotional dependency, economic and psychological abuse effectively traps survivors, stripping them of the agency required to leave abusive environments.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
295 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 173 0 R
/E (          iii. Cyber GBV / Online Harassment)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 173 0 R
/E (The rapid proliferation of digital technologies has given rise to Cyber GBV, a borderless threat that disproportionately targets women, girls, and human rights defenders. This includes the non-consensual sharing of intimate images, doxxing, cyberstalking, and organized online hate campaigns. Because digital harassment bypasses physical borders, member states face immense challenges in jurisdiction, digital policing, and content regulation, making cyber GBV a critical and evolving priority for modern international human rights forums.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 173 0 R
/E (          iv. Child, Early, and Forced Marriages \(CEFM\))
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 173 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 173 0 R
/E (The lack of robust domestic legal frameworks or police training in many member states leaves survivors without adequate protection, legal recourse, or safe shelters, perpetuating a cycle of impunity for perpetrators.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 174 0 R
/Pg 22 0 R
/A [715 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (            v. Harmful Traditional Practices)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
302 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (Harmful traditional practices represent violence justified under the guise of culture, tradition, or religion. This sub-category includes Female Genital Mutilation \(FGM\), honor killings, and dowry-related violence. SOCHUM maintains a strict stance that cultural relativism can never be invoked to justify the violation of basic human rights, and member states are continually urged to bridge the gap between international human rights standards and local customary laws.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
303 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 174 0 R
/T (     c\) The Socio-Economic Impact of Inequality)
/E (     c\) The Socio-Economic Impact of Inequality)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
304 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (The ramifications of gender inequality extend far beyond social injustice; they inflict profound damage on global macroeconomic development and public infrastructure. When women and girls are denied equal access to quality education, global economies lose half of their potential human capital, capping productivity and innovation. Furthermore, the pervasive "glass ceiling" and the gender pay gap prevent women from occupying leadership roles, stalling progress in both corporate and public sectors.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
305 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (A critical component of this economic disparity is the unequal distribution of unpaid care work. Globally, women perform the vast majority of unpaid domestic tasks, including childcare, eldercare, and household maintenance. This invisible labor force acts as a subsidy to the global economy but severely limits women's ability to participate in formal, paid employment or pursue higher education. Addressing these gaps is fundamental to fulfilling the UN's Sustainable Development Goals \(SDGs\), specifically SDG 5 \(Gender Equality\) and SDG 8 \(Decent Work and Economic Growth\).)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
306 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (     d\) Concept of Intersectionality)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
307 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
308 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 175 0 R
/Pg 23 0 R
/A [716 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
309 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (Intersectionality asserts that individuals do not experience discrimination in a vacuum; rather, gender inequality intersects with other forms of oppression, such as race, ethnicity, socioeconomic class, age, disability, geographic location, and migratory status. A singular focus on "women" as a homogenous group fails to account for the unique vulnerabilities faced by marginalized populations.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
310 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (For example, a displaced or refugee woman faces compound institutional barriers that a wealthy, native-born woman in a developed country does not. Similarly, indigenous women or women with disabilities experience significantly higher rates of GBV and encounter greater obstacles when trying to access legal justice or healthcare systems. For SOCHUM to draft effective resolutions, delegates must avoid "one-size-fits-all" approaches and instead design intersectional policies that actively accommodate and protect the most vulnerable sub-demographics of society.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
311 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 175 0 R
/T (6- RISKS and CURRENT CHALLANGES)
/E (6- RISKS and CURRENT CHALLANGES)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
312 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (     a\) Lack of Reporting and Social Stigma)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (Underreporting and social stigma remain among the most critical obstacles in effectively addressing gender-based violence \(GBV\) and protecting human rights globally. Stigma operates as a powerful mechanism of social control, often shifting the burden of blame from perpetrators to victims. Fear of public shaming, social ostracization, and family dishonor forces many survivors into silence, leaving severe human rights violations unaddressed within communities.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (This pervasive silence is further exacerbated by systemic distrust in law enforcement and legal frameworks. In many jurisdictions, inadequate victim-protection mechanisms, legislative loopholes, and a lack of gender-sensitive judicial training deter individuals from seeking justice.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
