/T (     b\) Weak Legal Frameworks and Impunity)
/E (     b\) Weak Legal Frameworks and Impunity)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
319 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 176 0 R
/T (A major impediment to eradicating gender-based violence \(GBV\) is the presence of inadequate legal frameworks and the widespread impunity enjoyed by perpetrators. In various legal systems globally, existing laws fail to explicitly criminalize all forms of violence, including marital rape, domestic abuse, and digital harassment. Furthermore, legislative ambiguity, outdated statutory definitions, and conflicting customary or religious practices often dilute formal legal protections, leaving survivors without effective recourse)
/E (A major impediment to eradicating gender-based violence \(GBV\) is the presence of inadequate legal frameworks and the widespread impunity enjoyed by perpetrators. In various legal systems globally, existing laws fail to explicitly criminalize all forms of violence, including marital rape, domestic abuse, and digital harassment. Furthermore, legislative ambiguity, outdated statutory definitions, and conflicting customary or religious practices often dilute formal legal protections, leaving survivors without effective recourse)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
320 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 176 0 R
/T (Even where comprehensive legislation exists, the enforcement gap remains substantial due to weak institutional capacity and systemic corruption. Law enforcement and judicial institutions frequently suffer from underfunding, insufficient training on gender sensitivity, and prolonged trial procedures. These structural flaws result in exceptionally low conviction rates, which reinforce a public perception that judicial systems are indifferent to survivors. By failing to hold perpetrators accountable, state institutions inadvertently sustain a cycle of violence, undermining rule of law and signaling that human rights violations against marginalized groups carry little to no consequence.)
/E (Even where comprehensive legislation exists, the enforcement gap remains substantial due to weak institutional capacity and systemic corruption. Law enforcement and judicial institutions frequently suffer from underfunding, insufficient training on gender sensitivity, and prolonged trial procedures. These structural flaws result in exceptionally low conviction rates, which reinforce a public perception that judicial systems are indifferent to survivors. By failing to hold perpetrators accountable, state institutions inadvertently sustain a cycle of violence, undermining rule of law and signaling that human rights violations against marginalized groups carry little to no consequence.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
