334 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 179 0 R
/T (The economic marginalization of women directly increases their vulnerability to systemic exploitation, poverty, and gender-based violence. Financial dependence on male family members or domestic partners often strips survivors of the financial means necessary to leave abusive relationships, secure independent housing, or access legal and medical aid. Furthermore, discriminatory practices regarding property ownership, inheritance laws, and access to financial credit continue to deny women economic self-sufficiency, entrenching generational cycles of poverty and economic vulnerability.)
/E (The economic marginalization of women directly increases their vulnerability to systemic exploitation, poverty, and gender-based violence. Financial dependence on male family members or domestic partners often strips survivors of the financial means necessary to leave abusive relationships, secure independent housing, or access legal and medical aid. Furthermore, discriminatory practices regarding property ownership, inheritance laws, and access to financial credit continue to deny women economic self-sufficiency, entrenching generational cycles of poverty and economic vulnerability.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
335 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 179 0 R
/T (Addressing these economic disparities is essential not only for achieving gender equality but also for fostering broader global economic resilience and sustainable development. When women lack equal access to financial resources and equal pay, entire economies suffer from lost productivity and underutilized human capital. Institutional reforms that enforce equal pay legislation, expand affordable childcare, promote financial inclusion, and protect informal workers are crucial steps toward closing the economic gender gap and ensuring long-term systemic stability)
/E (Addressing these economic disparities is essential not only for achieving gender equality but also for fostering broader global economic resilience and sustainable development. When women lack equal access to financial resources and equal pay, entire economies suffer from lost productivity and underutilized human capital. Institutional reforms that enforce equal pay legislation, expand affordable childcare, promote financial inclusion, and protect informal workers are crucial steps toward closing the economic gender gap and ensuring long-term systemic stability)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
